The BaseProc executors' twins (sprint 5, the refonte's job system): an experiment, NOT merged. It does not work, and the
reason is in the game, not in this patch.

What it does: HOS_EXECUTORS=<7..12> (with HOS_CORES at least as many) makes OoT's worker-set maker f_8069b020 create a
BaseProc executor on cores 6 and up as well. The game goes on registering its six for a stage; the host adds each one's
"twin" to the stage's scheduler (the list of contexts f_80b5ef60 makes for six gets 16 places), the worker's message
handler f_8004aae0 sends its twin the stage's message before it runs the dispatcher f_8004ab40 and waits for the twin's
end before it signals its own. That part works: with HOS_CORES=12 HOS_EXECUTORS=10 the stages run with 10 contexts
("threads: stage: ... registered 0x3f (6), 4 twins, scheduler ... holds 10 of 16") and the twins take jobs.

Why it fails: a twin crashes in its first actor job (BaseProcExecutor7, a read of [0 + 0x3e8] in f_8023b950 after
f_8022e220 returned 0). The game knows its job threads by identity, not by count: the BaseProc manager keeps the six
executors' thread ids ([manager + 128] + 32..52), each one's current BaseProc beside them (+56..), a seventh thread
(+104, +112), and the code that asks "which executor am I, and which BaseProc am I running" is inlined: a read of the
current thread from its TLS slot followed by the six compares is in 217 places of 152 functions (build/s5/idsites.py,
the strict count; 1671 places of 976 functions load those six words). A link from one BaseProc to another is refused to
a thread that is not one of the six (f_8022e220 returns 0), and the game's code does not expect the refusal on a job
thread. The current core is used the same way (193 functions read the "core + 1" TLS slot; per-core tables of six, with
`core < 6 ? core : 0`).
A twin that passed for its executor (same thread id, same core) would share that executor's "current BaseProc" and its
per-core tables with it while both run: the game's access checks and its lock-free per-core data would be wrong. More
identities would need six more places in those tables and every one of the inlined lookups rewritten.

So the six executors are a constant of the game. What the trace says there was to gain: all six run together for 2.3 ms
of a 11.9 ms frame (castle-move), and 0.8 ms with four at the end of the frame; the rest of the job stages is one to
five executors, held by the jobs' order.

